Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (f) The Company Board may, at any time prior to the receipt of the Company Stockholder Approval, make a recommendation change if the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that a failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. (g) The Company Board must consider not only the financial terms of an Acquisition Proposal but also the strategic benefits, potential regulatory hurdles, and the long-term impact on the Company's business operations, as advised by its legal and strategic advisors. (h) Any decision to recommend a Superior Proposal must take into account the likelihood of obtaining necessary regulatory approvals and the potential for integration challenges, as these factors could materially affect the long-term value and success of the proposal. (Pages 102-103)\nAnswer:
