Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.2. Strategic and Legal Considerations. <omitted> (b) The Company Board shall evaluate any Acquisition Proposal in accordance with this Agreement. Such evaluation must be conducted in good faith and after consultation with the Company’s strategic advisors, legal counsel, and financial advisors. The Board must determine whether the proposal aligns with the Company’s long-term strategic goals and complies with applicable regulatory requirements. (c) The Company Board shall not make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change unless the following conditions are met: (i) The Company Board determines that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal after considering strategic alignment, regulatory compliance, and financial terms; (ii) The Company has provided Parent with written notice of its intent to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change, including a detailed analysis of the strategic, legal, and financial aspects of the proposal; (iii) The Company has engaged in good faith negotiations with Parent to amend the terms of the current agreement to better align with the Company’s strategic objectives and fiduciary responsibilities. (d) Any amendment to the financial terms or any other material term of the Acquisition Proposal shall require a new notice and a three Business Day period for reevaluation. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
