Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may consider an Acquisition Proposal as a Superior Proposal if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and its financial advisor of national reputation, that the proposal would result in a transaction more favorable from a financial point of view to the Company’s shareholders than the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, (ii) the Company has given Parent written notice of the Company Board’s determination not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fourth Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, and (iii) after considering the results of negotiations with Parent, the Company Board shall have determined that the failure to accept the Superior Proposal would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. The Company Board must also consider strategic fit, potential synergies, and long-term growth prospects in making its determination. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer: 
